Refilter an open suggestion list while a keyboard composes on Android.

An Android keyboard composes whole words: every key while the word is
underlined is an update of one open composition. SuggestModel refilters an
open list on a content change only when the editor is not composing, and
does not follow the caret while it composes, so a list that was open when
the composition went on kept the items of the word as it stood when the
list was computed. Accepting an item replaces from the item's start to the
caret, getOverwriteInfo adding the columns the caret moved since, and by
then that covered every letter composed after the list was computed. On an
API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4, after a turn of the screen and back in a
file holding twox on another line, the list was open with twox focused
over line twox; y and z, composed into the same word, left it as it was,
and the key row's Tab turned line twoxyz into line twox, on the disk too,
in three runs of three. A hardware Enter did the same in two runs of two.
The keyboard's own Enter does not accept an item. In plain typing on a
fresh line the whole word was one composition and no list opened before
it ended, in three runs of three; the list came while a composition went
on after the turn, when the keyboard had reopened the word under the
caret and a letter went into it.

On Android a content change while composing now refilters an open list, as
a typed key does elsewhere: the list narrows to the composed word, a list
that opened by itself (a quick suggestion, a trigger character, the members
after a period) closes when nothing fits, and a deletion that moves the
caret left asks the providers again. Only an open session is refiltered:
_refilterWhileComposing returns when there is no trigger state, so a
composition update never opens a list, and one is armed at the
composition's end as before (patch 0025). An update that rewrites a word
with its own text, as a keyboard does when it reopens one, makes no edit
since patch 0048, so it raises no content change and does not touch the
list. Other platforms keep their behaviour: a desktop input method's
composition still waits for its end, where a dead key's lone accent cannot
be used for filtering.

The filter is the editor's: the word before the caret, matched character
by character. A Korean syllable being built is a jamo or part of a
syllable (ㅎ, then 하, then 한), which rarely starts a word of the file, so
a list that opened by itself closes at the first one that fits nothing,
and the syllable's end arms a new one as before. Japanese kana match only
kana words. A pinyin keyboard's Latin letters filter like any letters, so a
list can stay open over them. An item can still be accepted in the middle
of a composition, by Tab or a hardware Enter, as before; it now fits what
was composed, and the accept, an edit across the open composition,
restarts input (patch 0046), so the keyboard ends its composition at the
accepted text. A list opened by hand, with Ctrl+Space, that stops fitting
is frozen with its items, as on every platform, and Tab still accepts its
focused item over the composed word.

With the patch built, on the API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4 and WebView
134, the same steps kept line twoxyz in five runs of five, on the disk too,
the key row's Tab in three and a hardware Enter in two: the list was open
on twox after x and closed at y, where nothing in the file fits twoxy, so
the Tab indented and the Enter made a new line. Plain typing on a fresh
line opened no list in three runs of three. With a list open, a Backspace
inside the composing word widened it and a word tapped on the keyboard's
strip closed it, two runs each. In a file of 3002 words, ten letters
composed with a list open raised no long task in two runs, and the list's
new rows came 8 to 39 ms after each key as read by a poll every 25 ms; no
run without the patch was made on that file.

On an API 36 emulator with Gboard 18.5 and WebView 134, with a list opened
by hand, a Korean syllable was composed whole in three runs and a Japanese
word in three. In the Korean runs the list stayed frozen where nothing fit
and followed where an item did; in the Japanese runs it followed the word.
Nothing was accepted unasked, and no list opened during a composition with
none open before. The key row's Tab in the middle of a composition accepted
the focused item over the composed text, once on each keyboard, and input
restarted once.

Not measured: a member list that narrows under composed letters. Gboard
12.4 commits a letter typed right after a period without composing it, so
the list after a period was narrowed by the editor's ordinary path in three
runs of three.

